Compiler:
  - GCC (GNU Compiler Collection) is a compiler that translates C source code into machine code, producing an executable program.
  - Windows may not find gcc.exe because its folder(C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin) wasn’t in the PATH.

- Initialization > This is a very common programming pattern,
  which means giving a variable its first value before you start using it.

- #include > This is an instruction to the compiler.
  and #include <stdio.h> tells the compiler that before compiling the program, include this library.
  - <stdio.h>(Standard Input Ouput Header) > this is a header file(functionality) for working with input and output,
    printf() lives inside it, it also tells the compiler what its tools are, to translate to the computer,
    and this contains lot of tools, e.g. printf(), scanf(), ....

  - <stdlib.h>(standard library header) > 
  - <math.h>() > this contains a lot of mathematical operations like; sqrt(), ...

int main(){
  printf("Hello world\n");
  return 0;
} > 'int', this is telling that this function will return an integer.
  main(){}: This is a special function that every C program must define(the main function),
    without it the program wouldn't know where the execution should begin(start),
    and inside the {} is where the we write our codeblock for the function inwhich our execution will start from.
  printf(""); > printf(print formatted), to print something, and the data/value to be printed will be in a string,
    and the semicolon is telling the compiler that the particular line of code is finished to avoid errors.
  return 0; > this tells the operating system that the program finished successfully from what its function stated earlier.
  
// > this is used to create comment in C-programming, and /*multiple line of comment*/ for making multiple line of comment.

Placeholder:
  %d > This is a placeholder which is used to substitute a value,
    If used in a string it will be substituted by what value(integer) comes after the first comma of the string(called inline-var),
    and if a string contains two placeholder, it provided value after it will be substituted respectively.

  |  Format Specifier   |   Functionality                              |
  | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
  |       %d            | to interpret a value as integers(decimal).   |
  |       %lf           | to interpret a value as double(float num).   |
  |       %c            | to interpret a value as character.           |
  |       %f            | to interpret a value as float,               |
  |       %X            | to interpret a value as HexaDecimal.         |
  |       %%            | its one % when used with placeholders.       |
  |      %.1f           | to specify how many digit after the point.   |

Data Type:
  - int > Integer number(Decimal).
  - float > floating point number.
  - char  > A Character.


variable: We need to declare(create) our var before we can work(assignment) with it.
  Declaration > This is done by specifying it type and it name then end with a semicolon(int age;),
    var type 'double' is used to store floating point value.
  Assignment > this is done by taking the var name and assigning the matching type value to it(age = 30;).

Reading input:
  This is done by using the scanf() function,
   And inside it we provide a placeholder(to store the inputted value), then '&' key and an already created var name,
   the var is used so that the value will be assigned to it for usage,
   & > this is what assign the inputted value to the provided var.
   that is; char name; scanf("%d", &name);
  - A whitespace in the scanf format string tells scanf to skip any whitespace characters before reading the next input.
  - Each scanf simply reads what is currently waiting in the input buffer, from left to right.
  - Input stays in the input buffer until an input operation consumes it or the program ends.

Casting: Changing a particular data type to another type, putting new type in a () just before the prev type.
  1. when we make a mathematical operation btw numbers of the same type, we get the result of the same type.
  2. when we make a mathematical operation btw num of type int & float, we get the result of type float.

Swaping:
  This is the process of exchanging the value of two var by making the swaping process include an empty var.

Maths formular:
  1. T(F) = T(C) * 1.8 + 32
  2. Tn = a + (n - 1) d => n-term of an AP.
  3. Sn = n / 2 (2a + (n - 1) d) --OR-- Sn = n / 2 (a1 + Ln) => Sum of an AP.
  4. squareRoot((x1 - x2)^2 + (y1 - y2)^2) => Distance btw two points.
  5. 0 means False and non-zero(1, 5, or -1) means True.
  6. 4th quadrant < 3rd quadrant < 2nd quadrant < 1st quadrant : This is a point btw x and y axis, the quadrant going anti-clockwise.
  7. A year is a leap year! if it's divisible by 4 without remainder,
    and if it's divisible by 100 without a remainder, it's not a leap year! UNLESS
    it's also divisible by 400 without remainder, then it's a leap year!.

Condition: 
  - Relational Operators > Used to compare two values/operands and produce a true/false result(e.g. >, >=, <, <=, ==, != ).
  - Logical Operators > used to combine/manipulate two or more conditions/expressions and produce true/false results(e.g. &&, ||, !).
  - if statement > This is done by writing the keyword "if (condition) with a curlybraces{code block}"
   and the otherwise(else) will be as the "if" but without the (condition).
   if(int age > 20){printf("Go")}else{printf("Come")},
    this has two part; "if" is for if the condition is True & "else" is for if the condition is False.
  - Single quotes represent a single character ,WHILE, Double quotes represent a string(sequence of characters).
  

Switch case:
  This is a control mechanism which allows us to compare/check a given variable with multiple cases,
   and that variable must always be discrete(unique).
  Syntax:
    switch(varToCheck){ // code block
      case 'compareValue': // checking cases for where the varToCheck is == to the compareValue.
        // code block
        break;
      default: // this will be executed if the var(varToCheck) didn't match any of the cases.
        // code block
        break; // Used to stop execution from the switch case if a case(within it) is already executed.
    }

Ternary Operators:
  This takes three operands;
  - Conditions.
  - Code block for when the condition is True.
  - Code block for when the condition is False.
  Syntax: (Condition) ? Code for True : Code for False

Number Base:
  - Bit > This is a basic unit in computing used to represent a value,
    one binary digit, which can either be 0 or 1.

  - Byte > This(one byte) is a sequence(total) of 8 bits.

  - Binary is a base-2 number system, and a bit is one binary digit that can be either 0 or 1.

  - Base > This refers to how many different digits you have available before you need another position.
    Base 2(Binary num): is a number system that has only two available digits, 0 and 1, each position(digit^power) has a placed value based on the powers of 2.
    Base 10(Decimal num): is a number system that is available between 0 to 9, each position has a placed value based on the powers of 10.
    Base 16(HexaDecimal): is a number system that is available btw 0 to F(0-9 & capital A-F), each digit can represent 16 different values.
      - if we need to represent futher after F(15 in Decimal),
        then we will need 1(means 16 in HexaDecimal) with other Decimal digit to represent the Decimal value.
        e.g. 10(16 + 0) > 16(Decimal), 11(16 + 1) > 17(Decimal), ..., 1A(16 + 10) > 26(Decimal), ....
      - 0x > This is a notation for HexaDecimal, e.g, 0x2A3(HexaDecimal).
      - One HexaDecimal value represents 4 bit of a binary digit, with leading zeroes if not up to 4bit(0x2 > 0010 base2, 0xF > 1111 base2).

  - Digits > These are placed at "positions" from "right to left" starting at "position 0".

  - ASCII(American Standard Code for Information Interchange), uses number from 0 - 127: 
    This is a character encoding scheme that assigns a unique numerical code(decimal) to each character/digit/symbol in its character set,
      Encoding: Converting information into a particular format so it can be stored, processed, or communicated.
    * Things like;
      - Upper\Lower case letters ('A'-'Z' | 'a'-'z'),
      - Digits (0 - 9),
      - Punctuations (:,.,;,!, ...),
      - Other symbols (+, -, *, /),
      - Control characters (newline, tab) etc.
    - Each character(symbol) has its own associated(unique) encoded number in Decimal(OR in 0x and Binary).
    - '0'–'9' are 48–57(decimal)
      'A'–'Z' are 65–90(decimal)
      'a'–'z' are 97–122(decimal)
    - A character's ASCII code is fundamentally a number. %c,%d,%X simply tells printf to display that number as its character representation.

    - When converting a decimal number to binary using repeated division by 2:
      The remainder from the first division becomes the RIGHTMOST (least significant) bit of the binary result.

Loops:
  This is used to repeat a block of code based on a conditions.
  - while loop > This checks a condition BEFORE execution. #"Should I enter the loop?"
    Syntax: while(condition) { codeblock }.

  - do..while loop > This executes FIRST before checking its condition,
    then if it's True; it executes again ↺ until the condition becomes False.
    Syntax: do{codeblock}while(condition). #"I've entered the loop; should I repeat it?"
    
  - for loop(Counting loop) > This is used when the loop follows a knowm counting/repetition pattern, with three available section:
    * "Initialization" > Establishes the starting point,
      It is normally executed once, before the first iteration.
    * "Condition" > This is checked before each iteration, to either execute the codeblock or stop the loop.
    * "Update" > This can either be as an Increment/Decrement in the loop,
      it will be executed after each Iteration. And the condition is being checked again.
    Syntax: for(initialization; condition; update){codeblock}
    - break; > This is used to stop the execution of a loop, weather it has complete(iterations) the loop or not. 
    - continue; > This is used to skip the current iteration of a loop and not executing anything that comes after the continue;.
